data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_068ce09c874c9219006ad5e8cfd4f087d29c14f214e4f7e715","logprobs":[],"output_index":1,"sequence_number":439,"text":"## Hotels: version 1 vs. version 2\n\n- **Nightly maximums increased in version 2:** London £180 → **£200**; rest of UK £120 → **£130**; Lisbon or Dublin €170 → **€180**; other EU cities €150 → **€160**. Both versions say non-UK costs are converted at the rate on the day incurred.\n- **Booking and approval rules are largely unchanged:** Use the travel portal, book at least 14 days ahead where possible, and get written head-of-department approval for a last-minute booking over the limit. An off-portal booking is reimbursable only if the portal could not offer a comparable option, with an explanation in the claim. Version 2 calls it the “intranet travel portal.”\n- **Covered and excluded costs are the same:** The limit covers the room and taxes, but excludes minibar, excess room service, films, laundry for stays under five nights, and late checkout. Both versions allow a £25-per-night claim, with a receipt, when staying with friends or family instead. Self-booked upgrades are not reimbursed.\n- **Version 2 adds a rule for policy changes:** If a stay spans a policy change, each night is paid at the limit in force that night.\n- **Receipt threshold changed:** Version 1 requires an itemised receipt for a single expense over £50; version 2 requires one for each expense above £25. Claim-approval thresholds are otherwise the same.\n\nSources: *Travel and Expenses Policy, version 1.0*, Sections 3–5, 7–8; *Travel and Expenses Policy v2.0*, Sections 3–5, 7–8."}

